Progress status for this period: regressing. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your body is telling you it needs rest — take a deload this week before you dig yourself deeper.",
  "summary": "This was a rough week, and the data backs up exactly how you feel. Strength indices dropped across every single muscle group — some by as many as 7 points — while you were still putting in a solid 56 sets of work. That combination of high effort and declining performance is a classic sign that your body is running on empty. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI, you have every physical tool to build muscle well, but only if you give your system the chance to recover and adapt. The joint aches and that \"running on fumes\" feeling are not weaknesses — they are your body's warning signals, and they deserve to be taken seriously right now.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "Every muscle group regressed this period, and the drops are significant. Chest fell from 105 to 99 (down 6 points), back from 104 to 98 (down 6 points), and legs from 104 to 97 (down 7 points — the steepest decline). Shoulders dropped from 103 to 99 (down 4 points), triceps from 102 to 100 (down 2 points), and biceps from 101 to 100 (down 1 point). The threshold for flagging regression is a drop of 3 or more points, and four out of six muscle groups cleared that threshold. Crucially, this is not a programming failure — it is a recovery failure. You are still generating substantial tonnage (21,464 kg across 56 sets), which means the stimulus is there. The problem is that your body cannot absorb and convert that stimulus into strength and muscle while it is this fatigued. Continuing to push hard in this state will deepen the regression, not reverse it.",
    "status": "regressing"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps account for only 4% of total training volume, compared to 8.4% for triceps. For a muscle-building goal, direct biceps work is on the low side relative to triceps and to the overall arm development balance. This is worth addressing once recovery is restored."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs represent 22.4% of volume but recorded the largest strength index drop this period (7 points), suggesting they may be accumulating fatigue faster than they are recovering. Monitoring leg recovery closely after the deload will be important."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "All six muscle group strength indices declined this period, four of them by 3 or more points. Combined with the user's self-reported joint aches, general fatigue, and sessions that are getting progressively worse, the data strongly indicates accumulated systemic fatigue rather than a programming or effort problem. A deload — typically achieved by reducing training volume by around 40–50% and dropping intensity slightly while keeping movement patterns intact — will allow the nervous system and connective tissues to recover so that the following period can return to productive, upward progression.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Cut your total sets roughly in half next week, aiming for around 25–30 sets total, and reduce the weight on each lift to around 60–70% of what you have been using. The goal is to move, not to stress.",
    "Prioritise sleep above everything else this week. For a 28-year-old in active training, 8 or more hours of quality sleep per night is one of the most powerful recovery tools available and will accelerate how quickly you bounce back.",
    "Add a few minutes of light mobility or joint-friendly movement — such as bodyweight squats, hip circles, or arm swings — either at the start of sessions or on rest days, specifically to address the joint aches you reported.",
    "Once the deload is complete and you feel recovered, reintroduce progressive overload gradually — add weight or reps to just one or two lifts per session rather than across the board, so you can confirm your body is absorbing the training again before pushing harder."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "User experienced systemic regression across all muscle groups (chest -6, back -6, legs -7, shoulders -4) alongside self-reported joint pain and accumulated fatigue; a deload was recommended and volume was to be halved for the following period before resuming progressive overload."
}
